#!/usr/bin/env python3
# session-cursor -- deterministic in-session position resolver + set-result
# parser (lever b fast-path primitive).
#
# WHY: today the ONLY per-set model cost is the ENTRY turn -- the model wakes a
# full resume-context turn just to read "which slot / which set / how to parse
# '8 2'" from conversation memory and compose a set-add call. There is NO
# persisted session cursor (workouts has no current-slot pointer), so a bridge
# interceptor cannot build the set-add argv model-free. This CLI EXPOSES the
# position math set-table already computes (find_day_and_slot / fetch_current_
# sets) as a callable cursor, and folds the domain grammar (how "8 2" maps to
# reps/rir/weight for THIS slot) into deterministic code. That lets a bridge
# fast-path log a mid-slot numeric set with ZERO model tokens; anything the
# cursor cannot resolve deterministically returns FALLBACK -> normal model turn.
#
# NO new source of truth: day is read from workouts.session_type_id -> program
# day; slot order + planned sets from program.yaml; logged sets from
# workout_sets. This is the SAME state set-table renders from -- the cursor just
# reads it forward instead of drawing it.
#
# Ownership seam: the pack owns this DOMAIN primitive (grammar + gate live here).
# The framework owns the bridge interceptor that CONSUMES it -- the interceptor stays
# domain-agnostic: run `session-cursor parse ... --raw "<msg>"`, exec the emitted
# SETADD line on exit 0, else spawn the model turn.
#
# Usage:
#   session-cursor resolve [<session_id>] [--db P] [--program P] [--marker P]
#       Emit the current POSITION (machine key=value block). Answers from any
#       band -- working, ramp, prep, or "nothing logged yet" -- and states
#       separately, on the `fastpath=` line, whether that position is eligible
#       for the model-free fast path. exit 0 = position pinned; 2 = position
#       genuinely unknowable (FALLBACK reason on stdout); 1 = bad args /
#       missing files.
#       (2026-09-11: exit 2 no longer means "not fast-pathable". It used to,
#       and that is why a session whose only rows were ramp rungs read as
#       having no position at all -- see the band note above
#       _last_working_exercise.)
#
#   session-cursor parse [<session_id>] --raw "<msg>" [--db P] [--program P] [--marker P]
#       Parse a set-result message against the current cursor grammar. On a
#       deterministically-resolvable numeric set: print exactly one SETADD line =
#       the set-add argv the interceptor should run (exit 0). Otherwise print
#       "FALLBACK <reason>" (exit 2) -> route to the model.
#
#   session-cursor mark <session_id> [--marker P]     write the active-session marker
#   session-cursor clear [--marker P]                 remove the active-session marker
#   session-cursor get [--marker P]                   print active session id (exit 0) or exit 2
#
# session_id defaults to the active-session marker (files/program/.active-session)
# when omitted. Marker missing AND no id -> FALLBACK no_active_session.
#
# Reuse: imports set-table (same dir, no .py ext) via importlib for
# find_day_and_slot / fetch_current_sets / load_rx_calc; set-table's __main__
# guard means import runs no side effects.
#
# stdlib only. ASCII source.

